ices Provided\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Understand transport layer primitives, Berkeley sockets, and end-to-end communication.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Interactive Lecture\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Writing basic socket programming flow charts.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Socket flow quiz\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Flowchart creation\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 38,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Elements of Transport Protocols - Connection Establishment\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Analyze three-way handshake and connection release mechanisms.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Chalk and Talk\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Drawing timeline diagrams for connection setup and teardown.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Handshake sequence check\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Timeline roleplay\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 39,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Elements of Transport Protocols - Flow Control and Buffering\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Study transport-level flow control, multiplexing, and crash recovery.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Interactive Lecture\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Analyzing sliding window at transport layer vs data link layer.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Comparison quiz\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Discussion on layer differences\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 40,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Congestion Control - Principles and Policies\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Understand the principles of congestion control in the transport layer.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Interactive Lecture\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Comparing open-loop and closed-loop congestion control.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Concept quiz\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Group discussion\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 41,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"UDP (User Datagram Protocol)\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Analyze UDP header format, pseudo-header checksum, and use cases.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Wireshark Protocol Analysis\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Capturing and inspecting UDP packets in Wireshark.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Wireshark Lab Report part 1\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Hands-on packet capture\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 42,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) - Header and Services\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Examine TCP segment structure, flags, and reliable data transfer.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Wireshark Protocol Analysis\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Examining TCP flags (SYN, ACK, FIN, RST) in Wireshark traces.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Wireshark Lab Report part 2\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Trace inspection exercise\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 43,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"TCP Connection Management\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Trace TCP state machine through connection lifecycle.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Interactive Lecture\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Mapping TCP states to connection events.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"State machine quiz\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"State tracking activity\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 44,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"TCP Transmission Policy and Timer Management\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Understand TCP sliding window, Nagle\'s algorithm, and retransmission timers.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Chalk and Talk\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Calculating RTT and Karn\'s algorithm implementation.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Timer calculation check\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Problem-solving\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 45,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"TCP Congestion Control - Slow Start and Congestion Avoidance\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Analyze TCP congestion control algorithms (Slow Start, Congestion Avoidance).\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Wireshark Protocol Analysis\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Plotting congestion window size growth from Wireshark captures.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Congestion graph evaluation\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Graph analysis\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 46,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"TCP Congestion Control - Fast Recovery and Fast Retransmit\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Examine fast retransmit, fast recovery, and modern TCP variants (CUBIC, BBR).\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Interactive Lecture\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Comparing TCP Tahoe and Reno behaviors.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Quiz on TCP variants\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Comparative discussion\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 47,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Transport Layer Troubleshooting\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Diagnose network performance issues using transport layer metrics.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Wireshark Protocol Analysis\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Analyzing retransmissions and out-of-order packets in Wireshark.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Wireshark Lab Report final submission\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Troubleshooting lab\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 48,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Transport Layer Review and Wrap-up\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Synthesize UDP and TCP mechanisms and congestion control strategies.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Interactive Lecture\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Review quiz and Q&A session for Unit 4.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Unit 4 formative test\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Interactive quiz\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 49,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"DNS (Domain Name System) - Architecture and Operation\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Understand DNS hierarchy, root servers, TLDs, and recursive\\/iterative queries.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"PPT Presentation\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Tracing a DNS query using command-line tools (nslookup\\/dig).\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"DNS query worksheet\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Command-line exploration\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 50,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"DNS - Records and Security (DNSSEC)\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Examine DNS resource records (A, CNAME, MX, NS) and DNSSEC.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Demonstration of DNS lookup and HTTP traffic\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Inspecting DNS records and caching behavior.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Quiz on DNS records\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Live traffic demonstration\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 51,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Electronic Mail - SMTP, POP3, and IMAP\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Analyze email architecture, SMTP commands, and mailbox access protocols.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Interactive Lecture\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Simulating SMTP dialogue via telnet.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Assignment 3 part A\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Simulated protocol session\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 52,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Electronic Mail - MIME and Webmail\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Understand MIME extensions for non-ASCII data and web-based email.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"PPT Presentation\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Examining email headers for MIME parts.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Header analysis check\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Header inspection exercise\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 53,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"World Wide Web - HTTP Architecture\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Examine HTTP\\/1.0, HTTP\\/1.1, persistent\\/non-persistent connections, and methods.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Demonstration of DNS lookup and HTTP traffic\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Capturing HTTP request and response headers in browser developer tools.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"HTTP traffic quiz\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Browser developer tools lab\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 54,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"World Wide Web - Cookies, Caching, and HTTP\\/2\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Understand web caching, cookies, sessions, and HTTP\\/2 improvements.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Interactive Lecture\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Discussing privacy implications of cookies and tracking.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Assignment 3 submission\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Privacy discussion panel\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 55,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Cryptography Basics - Symmetric Encryption\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Understand substitution\\/transposition ciphers, DES, and AES basics.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Chalk and Talk\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Manual encryption\\/decryption using basic ciphers.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Cipher puzzle check\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Cipher cracking activity\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 56,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Cryptography Basics - Asymmetric Encryption\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Examine public-key cryptography (RSA algorithm basics) and key exchange.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"PPT Presentation\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Simple modular arithmetic calculations for RSA.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"RSA math check\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Math problem sprint\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 57,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Digital Signatures and Authentication\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Understand digital signatures, hash functions (SHA), and certificates (PKI).\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Interactive Lecture\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Tracing certificate validation in a web browser.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"PKI quiz\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Certificate inspection\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 58,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Network Security - Firewalls and VPNs\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Explore packet filtering routers, application gateways, stateful firewalls, and VPNs.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"PPT Presentation\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Designing a basic firewall rule set for an enterprise network.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Firewall rule exercise\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Rule design challenge\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 59,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Application Layer & Security Review\\\",\\n    \\\"duration_mins\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"objectives\\\": [\\n      \\\"Synthesize application layer protocols and network security mechanisms.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"teaching_method\\\": \\\"Interactive Lecture\\\",\\n    \\\"activities\\\": [\\n      \\\"Comprehensive review quiz covering Unit 5.\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"formative_assessment\\\": [\\n      \\\"Quiz Unit 5\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"engagement\\\": [\\n      \\\"Review game\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"session_number\\\": 60,\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Comprehensive Course Review and Exam Prep\\\",\\n    \\\"duration\",\"error\":null}','gemini-3.5-flash-lite',NULL,NULL,18789,'success',NULL,'course_plan',2,'2026-11-12 03:32:41'),(9,1,3,'ppt','ppt','{\"title\":\"Computer Networks\",\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"unit\":1,\"provider\":\"gemini\"}','[{\"number\":1,\"title\":\"Computer Networks: Full Course Lecture\",\"layout\":\"content\",\"bullets\":[\"Institution: K.L.N College of Engineering\",\"Department: Computer Science and Engineering\",\"Course: Computer Networks\",\"Scope: Comprehensive Full-Course Coverage (Units 1 to 5)\"],\"speaker_notes\":\"Welcome to the comprehensive lecture on Computer Networks for Computer Science and Engineering students at K.L.N College of Engineering. Today we cover the entire syllabus across all five units.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Introduction\"},{\"number\":2,\"title\":\"Course Learning Objectives\",\"layout\":\"content\",\"bullets\":[\"Analyze network architectures using OSI and TCP\\/IP reference models.\",\"Evaluate data link layer framing, error control, and medium access control protocols.\",\"Design routing algorithms and examine IPv4\\/IPv6 addressing schemes.\",\"Compare transport layer protocols (TCP vs. UDP) and congestion control mechanisms.\",\"Implement core application layer services and basic network security principles.\"],\"speaker_notes\":\"Review these specific objectives with the class, emphasizing measurable competencies in protocol analysis, routing design, and security basics.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Introduction\"},{\"number\":3,\"title\":\"Unit 1: Network Architecture and Reference Models\",\"layout\":\"comparison\",\"bullets\":[\"Network Hardware: LANs, WANs, switches, routers, and transmission media (guided\\/unguided).\",\"OSI Model: 7 distinct layers separating physical signaling from application logic.\",\"TCP\\/IP Model: 4-layer architecture optimized for Internet engineering.\"],\"comparison\":{\"headers\":[\"Feature\",\"OSI Model\",\"TCP\\/IP Model\"],\"rows\":[[\"Layers\",\"7 Layers\",\"4 Layers\"],[\"Protocol Dependency\",\"Strictly defines services, interfaces, and protocols\",\"Protocols came first; model was fitted to them\"],[\"Transport Layer\",\"Connection-oriented strictly separated\",\"Supports both TCP and UDP prominently\"]]},\"speaker_notes\":\"Explain the structural differences between OSI and TCP\\/IP, emphasizing how physical and data link layers handle local transmission while network and transport handle end-to-end delivery.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Unit 1\"},{\"number\":4,\"title\":\"Unit 2: Data Link Layer & Medium Access Control\",\"layout\":\"content\",\"bullets\":[\"Design Issues: Framing, error control, and flow control.\",\"Error Detection\\/Correction: Hamming codes and Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC).\",\"Sliding Window Protocols: Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat for reliable delivery.\",\"Multiple Access Protocols: ALOHA, CSMA\\/CD (Ethernet), and CSMA\\/CA.\"],\"speaker_notes\":\"Discuss how the Data Link layer ensures reliable transfer over unreliable physical links. Compare Go-Back-N with Selective Repeat retransmission overhead.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Unit 2\"},{\"number\":5,\"title\":\"Unit 3: Network Layer: Routing and Addressing\",\"layout\":\"content\",\"bullets\":[\"Routing Algorithms: Distance Vector (Bellman-Ford) vs. Link State (Dijkstra\'s).\",\"Congestion Control: Leaky bucket and token bucket traffic shaping algorithms.\",\"Quality of Service (QoS): Techniques for managing jitter, bandwidth, and latency.\",\"IP Addressing: IPv4 subnetting\\/CIDR notation and IPv6 128-bit address architecture.\"],\"speaker_notes\":\"Explain how Dijkstra\'s algorithm computes shortest paths in Link State routing, and contrast IPv4 address exhaustion with IPv6 expansion.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Unit 3\"},{\"number\":6,\"title\":\"Unit 4: Transport Layer Protocols\",\"layout\":\"comparison\",\"bullets\":[\"Transport Service: Provides logical communication between application processes.\",\"Congestion Control: AIMD (Additive Increase Multiplicative Decrease) and slow start.\",\"UDP: Connectionless, unreliable, low-overhead datagram service.\",\"TCP: Connection-oriented, reliable, byte-stream service with 3-way handshake.\"],\"comparison\":{\"headers\":[\"Parameter\",\"TCP\",\"UDP\"],\"rows\":[[\"Connection\",\"Connection-oriented\",\"Connectionless\"],[\"Reliability\",\"High (Acknowledgements & retransmissions)\",\"Best-effort (No guarantees)\"],[\"Use Case\",\"Web (HTTP), Email (SMTP), File Transfer\",\"Streaming, DNS, VoIP\"]]},\"speaker_notes\":\"Walk through the TCP 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) and explain why streaming applications prefer UDP over TCP.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Unit 4\"},{\"number\":7,\"title\":\"Unit 5: Application Layer and Network Security\",\"layout\":\"content\",\"bullets\":[\"DNS: Hierarchical naming system resolving domain names to IP addresses.\",\"Email Protocols: SMTP for mail transfer; IMAP for mailbox synchronization.\",\"Web Architecture: HTTP request\\/response methods and stateless protocol design.\",\"Cryptography & Security: Symmetric\\/asymmetric encryption, digital signatures, and firewalls.\"],\"speaker_notes\":\"Detail how DNS operates recursively and iteratively. Explain the role of public-key cryptography in securing web traffic via TLS\\/SSL.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Unit 5\"},{\"number\":8,\"title\":\"Unit Summary & Revision Questions\",\"layout\":\"summary\",\"bullets\":[\"Unit 1-2: Mastered physical-to-link layer framing, media access, and reference models.\",\"Unit 3-4: Explored IP routing, QoS, and transport reliability via TCP\\/UDP mechanisms.\",\"Unit 5: Examined application services (DNS, HTTP) and fundamental security practices.\",\"Revision Q1: Calculate the CRC remainder for a given bit stream and generator polynomial.\",\"Revision Q2: Compare distance vector routing routing loops with link state convergence.\"],\"speaker_notes\":\"Use these revision questions for an interactive review session before exams.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Conclusion\"},{\"number\":9,\"title\":\"Thank You\",\"layout\":\"close\",\"bullets\":[\"Department of Computer Science and Engineering\",\"K.L.N College of Engineering\",\"Open for Questions and Discussion\"],\"speaker_notes\":\"Thank the students and open the floor for any final questions regarding the computer networks syllabus.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Conclusion\"}]','gemini-3.5-flash-lite',NULL,NULL,5589,'success',NULL,'presentation',2,'2026-11-12 03:33:16'),(10,1,3,'assignment','assignment','{\"type\":\"case_study\",\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"class_id\":1,\"provider\":\"gemini\"}','{\"title\":\"Case Study: Enterprise Network Redesign and Troubleshooting\",\"description\":\"Global Logistics Corp. is experiencing severe network latency, packet loss, and security vulnerabilities across its headquarters and three regional branches. The current flat network topology causes broadcast storms, and the lack of subnetting leads to IP exhaustion. As the Lead Network Architect, you are required to analyze the existing network bottlenecks, propose an optimized hierarchical IP addressing and routing scheme, implement secure VLAN configurations, and diagnose specific routing protocol failures.\",\"instructions\":[\"Analyze the provided network topology and traffic bottleneck logs for Global Logistics Corp.\",\"Propose a redesigned hierarchical IP addressing scheme using VLSM to optimize subnet allocation.\",\"Configure VLANs and inter-VLAN routing to segment traffic and mitigate broadcast storms.\",\"Identify and troubleshoot the OSPF routing loop and convergence issues present in the branch offices.\",\"Submit a comprehensive report including network diagrams, IP allocation tables, configuration snippets, and justification for the security measures implemented.\"],\"max_marks\":50,\"rubric\":[{\"criterion\":\"Network Problem Analysis\",\"description\":\"Accurately identify the root causes of latency, broadcast storms, and IP exhaustion from the given scenario.\",\"marks\":10,\"clo\":\"CLO1\",\"bloom\":\"K2\",\"levels\":[\"Poor: Fails to identify core issues\",\"Average: Identifies some issues with minor gaps\",\"Excellent: Thoroughly analyzes and categorizes all network bottlenecks\"]},{\"criterion\":\"IP Addressing and VLSM Design\",\"description\":\"Design an optimal hierarchical IP addressing scheme using Variable Length Subnet Masking (VLSM).\",\"marks\":10,\"clo\":\"CLO2\",\"bloom\":\"K3\",\"levels\":[\"Poor: Major calculation errors or inefficient IP usage\",\"Average: Mostly correct subnetting with minor inefficiencies\",\"Excellent: Flawless VLSM design meeting all constraints and future scalability\"]},{\"criterion\":\"VLAN and Inter-VLAN Routing Implementation\",\"description\":\"Configure VLANs, trunking, and router-on-a-stick or multilayer switching for traffic segmentation.\",\"marks\":10,\"clo\":\"CLO3\",\"bloom\":\"K3\",\"levels\":[\"Poor: Incorrect VLAN assignments or routing configuration\",\"Average: Functional configuration with missing security best practices\",\"Excellent: Robust and secure VLAN implementation with proper inter-VLAN routing\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Routing Protocol Troubleshooting\",\"description\":\"Diagnose OSPF routing loops, convergence delays, and propose corrective configurations.\",\"marks\":10,\"clo\":\"CLO4\",\"bloom\":\"K3\",\"levels\":[\"Poor: Unable to locate the routing failure\",\"Average: Identifies the issue but provides an incomplete fix\",\"Excellent: Accurately pinpoints the routing anomaly and provides an optimal solution\"]},{\"criterion\":\"Security and Comprehensive Reporting\",\"description\":\"Integrate security policies (ACLs) and present the final architectural report clearly and professionally.\",\"marks\":10,\"clo\":\"CLO5\",\"bloom\":\"K3\",\"levels\":[\"Poor: Lacks security considerations and poor report structure\",\"Average: Basic security measures included with adequate presentation\",\"Excellent: Comprehensive security integration with clear diagrams and professional documentation\"]}]}','gemini-3.5-flash-lite',NULL,NULL,3637,'success',NULL,'assignment',2,'2026-11-12 03:34:38'),(11,1,3,'questions','questions','{\"type\":\"short\",\"unit\":1,\"klevel\":\"K2\",\"count\":5,\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"provider\":\"gemini\"}','[{\"stem\":\"Explain the primary difference between the OSI reference model and the TCP\\/IP reference model regarding their layer structures, providing an example of how a function in the transport layer is handled differently or similarly in both.\",\"options\":null,\"correct_answer\":\"The OSI model has 7 layers, separating the session and presentation layers distinctly from the application layer, whereas TCP\\/IP collapses these into a single application layer (4 layers total). Despite this, both models implement reliable end-to-end data transport (such as TCP) at their respective transport layers.\",\"explanation\":\"K2 requires explaining conceptual differences with examples. The student must demonstrate understanding of the structural divergence (7 vs 4 layers) and how protocol functions like reliable transport map across both architectures.\",\"marks\":5,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"Contrast guided transmission media with unguided transmission media by explaining how each guides signal propagation, and provide a real-world enterprise network scenario where combining both media types is necessary.\",\"options\":null,\"correct_answer\":\"Guided media (like fiber optics or twisted-pair copper) physically constrain electromagnetic signals along a solid path, offering high bandwidth and security. Unguided media (like wireless radio waves or microwaves) propagate signals through free space without a physical conductor, allowing mobility. An example scenario is a corporate office using fiber optics for high-speed backbone connections between wired desktop workstations and Wi-Fi access points for mobile laptop users.\",\"explanation\":\"This tests the student\'s ability to contrast physical propagation mechanisms and apply them to a practical network topology example.\",\"marks\":5,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"Describe the functional relationship between network software (specifically protocol stacks and operating systems) and network hardware (such as Network Interface Cards and routers). Give an example of how they interact when a packet is transmitted.\",\"options\":null,\"correct_answer\":\"Network hardware provides the physical components for signal transmission and reception, while network software (protocols and drivers) dictates how data is formatted, addressed, and controlled. For example, when an application sends data, the operating system\'s protocol stack encapsulates the data into packets and passes it to the NIC hardware, which translates those packets into electrical or optical signals for transmission over the physical medium.\",\"explanation\":\"The question targets the hardware-software interaction in Unit 1, requiring an explanation of encapsulation and hardware translation with an example.\",\"marks\":5,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"Explain the concept of resource sharing as a primary use of computer networks, and distinguish between hardware resource sharing and software resource sharing using a campus network example.\",\"options\":null,\"correct_answer\":\"Resource sharing allows geographically distributed users to access common devices and programs efficiently. Hardware sharing involves multiple users utilizing a single physical device, such as a university department sharing a centralized high-end network printer. Software sharing involves accessing programs or data remotely, such as students accessing a centralized database license server or cloud-hosted integrated development environment over the campus network.\",\"explanation\":\"Evaluates the conceptual understanding of network uses (resource sharing) and requires clear examples differentiating hardware vs software domains.\",\"marks\":5,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"Explain the role of the Physical Layer in the OSI model, focusing on how it handles bit representation, and give an example of how a digital bitstream is transformed for transmission over a copper cable.\",\"options\":null,\"correct_answer\":\"The Physical Layer is responsible for transmitting raw unstructured bits over a physical communication medium, managing voltage levels, data rates, and physical connectors. For example, to transmit a digital bitstream over a twisted-pair copper cable, the physical layer translates binary 1s and 0s into specific voltage fluctuations or electrical pulses (such as using Manchester encoding) so the receiving hardware can reliably interpret the signals.\",\"explanation\":\"Tests K2 understanding of the physical layer\'s core responsibility\\u2014bit transmission\\u2014and requires explaining the translation mechanism with an example.\",\"marks\":5,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1}]','gemini-3.5-flash-lite',NULL,NULL,4212,'success',NULL,'question_bank',2,'2026-11-12 03:37:49'),(12,1,3,'questions','questions','{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"unit\":1,\"klevel\":\"K2\",\"count\":5,\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"provider\":\"gemini\"}','{\"questions\":[{\"stem\":\"How does the OSI reference model\'s Data Link layer handle error control differently than the Transport layer?\",\"options\":{\"A\":\"The Data Link layer provides node-to-node error detection and correction over a single physical link, whereas the Transport layer ensures end-to-end data integrity and reliability across the entire network path.\",\"B\":\"The Data Link layer retransmits lost packets across the entire internetwork, while the Transport layer only checks bit errors on the immediate physical wire.\",\"C\":\"The Data Link layer relies entirely on upper layers for framing and error detection, leaving the Transport layer responsible for MAC-address-based delivery.\",\"D\":\"The Data Link layer operates strictly in stateless mode without acknowledgments, while the Transport layer performs physical-medium modulation checks.\"},\"correct_answer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"At K2 level, understanding reference models requires distinguishing the scope of responsibilities between layers. The Data Link layer manages hop-by-hop framing and error control, while the Transport layer manages end-to-end reliability between source and destination hosts.\",\"marks\":1,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"Why are multi-mode optical fibers typically used for short-range campus networks while single-mode fibers are preferred for long-distance telecommunications?\",\"options\":{\"A\":\"Multi-mode fiber has a larger core that allows multiple light modes to propagate, leading to modal dispersion over long distances, whereas single-mode fiber has a narrower core that supports a single light path with minimal dispersion.\",\"B\":\"Multi-mode fiber uses expensive laser diodes that can only transmit over short distances, whereas single-mode fiber uses cheap LEDs suitable for trans-oceanic cables.\",\"C\":\"Multi-mode fiber completely eliminates signal attenuation through amplification, while single-mode fiber suffers from high electromagnetic interference from power lines.\",\"D\":\"Multi-mode fiber operates exclusively on the principle of electrical conductivity, whereas single-mode fiber relies on wireless radio frequency coupling.\"},\"correct_answer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"This tests the understanding of guided transmission media characteristics. Core size and light propagation modes dictate that multi-mode suffers from modal dispersion over long runs, making single-mode necessary for high-bandwidth, long-distance links.\",\"marks\":1,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"In the context of network software architecture, what is the primary structural difference between protocol stacks and layered operating systems?\",\"options\":{\"A\":\"Protocol stacks organize network functions into a vertical sequence of layers where each layer adds its own header to provide specific communication services, whereas traditional OS layering structures internal hardware management.\",\"B\":\"Protocol stacks combine all seven OSI layers into a single monolithic module for faster CPU execution, while layered operating systems separate network tasks into user-space applications.\",\"C\":\"Protocol stacks are hardware circuits embedded permanently in the NIC, whereas network software architecture refers exclusively to cloud storage protocols.\",\"D\":\"Protocol stacks eliminate the need for addressing by broadcasting all packets, whereas layered operating systems use centralized routing tables.\"},\"correct_answer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"K2 assessment of network software requires explaining how layering structures communication protocols through encapsulation and modular service provision, distinguishing it from general software constructs.\",\"marks\":1,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"How do connection-oriented services in the TCP\\/IP suite differ conceptually from connectionless services?\",\"options\":{\"A\":\"Connection-oriented services establish a formal virtual circuit or handshake before data transfer to guarantee ordered delivery, whereas connectionless services treat each packet independently without prior setup.\",\"B\":\"Connection-oriented services route packets through dedicated physical copper wires for the duration of the session, whereas connectionless services use optical fiber exclusively.\",\"C\":\"Connection-oriented services do not use sequence numbers because reliability is handled entirely by the physical layer, whereas connectionless services require complex handshakes.\",\"D\":\"Connection-oriented services are restricted to local area networks, whereas connectionless services are only applicable to peer-to-peer file sharing.\"},\"correct_answer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"This evaluates the conceptual understanding of network services. Connection-oriented protocols (like TCP) maintain state and sequencing, whereas connectionless protocols (like UDP\\/IP) route datagrams independently.\",\"marks\":1,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"When comparing unguided transmission media, why is line-of-sight propagation critical for microwave communication frequencies above 10 GHz?\",\"options\":{\"A\":\"Higher frequency waves are easily blocked by physical obstacles and experience severe atmospheric absorption, preventing reliable diffraction around the curvature of the Earth.\",\"B\":\"Higher frequency signals require physical metallic wave-guides laid out along the ground to prevent them from escaping into the upper atmosphere.\",\"C\":\"Higher frequency radio waves travel faster than the speed of light in a vacuum, requiring direct alignment to prevent temporal distortion.\",\"D\":\"Higher frequency transmissions automatically convert into guided infrared beams once they travel more than ten meters from the transmitter.\"},\"correct_answer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Testing unguided media properties at K2 level involves explaining how frequency bands dictate propagation methods, specifically why high-frequency microwaves demand unobstructed line-of-sight paths.\",\"marks\":1,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1}]}','gemini-3.5-flash-lite',NULL,NULL,5335,'success',NULL,'question_bank',3,'2026-11-12 03:39:07'),(13,1,3,'questions','questions','{\"type\":\"long\",\"unit\":1,\"klevel\":\"K2\",\"count\":5,\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"provider\":\"gemini\"}','[{\"stem\":\"Compare and contrast the OSI reference model and the TCP\\/IP reference model in terms of their layer structures, design philosophies, and how they handle the transport and network layers. Provide specific architectural examples for each model to illustrate your explanation.\",\"options\":[],\"correct_answer\":\"The OSI model is a 7-layer conceptual framework developed by ISO, strictly separating concerns with distinct presentation and session layers, but can be rigid. The TCP\\/IP model is a 4-layer pragmatic suite developed by DoD, combining presentation and session into the application layer and keeping link-layer details separate from internetworking. For example, TCP\\/IP successfully integrated IP as a universal network-layer protocol across diverse hardware, whereas OSI\'s rigorous theoretical approach often led to over-engineered specifications.\",\"explanation\":\"This question requires a comprehensive K2-level explanation mapping structural differences and practical examples between OSI and TCP\\/IP without circular definitions.\",\"marks\":10,\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"Explain the fundamental differences between guided (wired) and unguided (wireless) transmission media, focusing on twisted-pair cables, coaxial cables, fiber optics, and radio\\/microwave propagation. Include real-world operational examples and limitations for each medium.\",\"options\":[],\"correct_answer\":\"Guided media use physical conductors to confine signals, such as copper strands in twisted-pair and coaxial cables for local area networks and cable television, or glass cores in fiber optics for high-speed, long-distance backbone connections utilizing total internal reflection. Unguided media transmit electromagnetic waves through free space via antennas, such as terrestrial microwave and radio frequencies used in Wi-Fi and cellular networks, which are subject to environmental interference, attenuation, and security vulnerabilities compared to physical cables.\",\"explanation\":\"Tests understanding of physical layer transmission characteristics, operational principles, and practical trade-offs.\",\"marks\":10,\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"Discuss the architectural concept of protocol layering and service interfaces in computer networks. Explain how peer-to-peer communication occurs across different layers using headers, encapsulation, and decapsulation, supported by a concrete end-to-end data transfer example.\",\"options\":[],\"correct_answer\":\"Protocol layering breaks complex network communication into manageable, modular vertical tiers where each layer provides services to the layer above it while hiding implementation details. As data moves down the sender\'s stack, each layer prepends its own control header via encapsulation. Upon reaching the receiver, the peer layer strips its respective header via decapsulation. For instance, an HTTP request gets a TCP segment header, then an IP packet header, and finally a data-link frame header before physical transmission, reversing the process at the destination.\",\"explanation\":\"Evaluates the candidate\'s conceptual grasp of modular network software design and data encapsulation mechanics.\",\"marks\":10,\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"Examine the classification of computer networks based on their geographical scale, specifically contrasting Local Area Networks (LANs), Metropolitan Area Networks (MANs), and Wide Area Networks (WANs). Discuss the typical hardware components, transmission technologies, and ownership models associated with each category.\",\"options\":[],\"correct_answer\":\"LANs cover a single room, building, or campus, utilizing high-speed Ethernet switches and private coaxial or twisted-pair media with high data rates and low delay, typically owned by a single organization. MANs span an entire city or town, often bridging multiple LANs using technologies like fiber-optic rings or carrier Ethernet. WANs span countries or continents, interconnecting disparate networks via leased telecommunication lines, routers, satellite links, and optical transport networks, often managed by multiple telecom service providers.\",\"explanation\":\"Tests network topologies and geographical categorizations alongside underlying hardware architectures.\",\"marks\":10,\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"Explain the role and functionality of core network hardware devices\\u2014including repeaters, hubs, bridges, switches, routers, and gateways\\u2014within the context of the physical and data link layers of the OSI reference model. Provide examples of how each device manages collision domains and broadcast domains.\",\"options\":[],\"correct_answer\":\"Repeaters and hubs operate at the physical layer, blindly amplifying or repeating electrical signals across all ports, thus sharing a single collision and broadcast domain. Bridges and layer-2 switches operate at the data link layer, inspecting MAC addresses to forward frames selectively, thereby segmenting collision domains while typically maintaining a single broadcast domain. Routers operate at the network layer using IP addresses to route packets between different networks, breaking up both collision and broadcast domains. Gateways operate across multiple layers to perform protocol translation between incompatible architectures.\",\"explanation\":\"Requires clear articulation of network device functionalities, OSI layer placement, and domain segmentation concepts.\",\"marks\":10,\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1}]','gemini-3.5-flash-lite',NULL,NULL,6233,'success',NULL,'question_bank',4,'2026-11-12 03:39:44'),(14,1,3,'questions','questions','{\"type\":\"essay\",\"unit\":1,\"klevel\":\"K2\",\"count\":5,\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"provider\":\"gemini\"}','{\"questions\":[{\"stem\":\"Compare and contrast the OSI reference model and the TCP\\/IP reference model. Explain two structural similarities and two key architectural differences between them, providing a practical networking example for each difference.\",\"correct_answer\":\"Model outline: Similarities include layered architecture promoting modularity and having distinct transport and network layers. Differences include OSI having 7 layers with strict service\\/protocol separation while TCP\\/IP has 4 layers blending presentation\\/session into application and data link\\/physical into link layer. Examples should illustrate how TCP\\/IP bundles protocols like HTTP\\/TLS\\/DNS into the application layer compared to OSI\'s distinct layers.\",\"marks\":10,\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"Explain the fundamental differences between guided (wired) and unguided (wireless) transmission media in terms of signal propagation, attenuation, and susceptibility to environmental interference. Provide real-world networking scenarios where each medium is uniquely advantageous.\",\"correct_answer\":\"Model outline: Guided media (e.g., twisted pair, fiber optics) constrain signals within a physical path, typically offering higher bandwidth and lower interference, ideal for high-speed local data centers. Unguided media (e.g., radio waves, microwaves) propagate signals through free space, susceptible to atmospheric attenuation, multipath fading, and interference, but essential for mobile and remote connectivity.\",\"marks\":10,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1},{\"stem\":\"Discuss the hierarchical organization of network hardware and software. Explain the roles of edge devices (hosts), intermediate devices (routers and switches), and how the operating system\'s network stack interacts with protocol software to move data across a network.\",\"correct_answer\":\"Model outline: Edge devices generate and consume user data. Intermediate devices forward packets across network boundaries using switching and routing functions. Software architecture separates user applications from kernel-level protocol stacks, where data is encapsulated as it descends through layers and decapsulated upon receipt, demonstrating clean separation of concerns.\",\"marks\":10,\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"bloom_k_level\":\"K2\",\"unit_number\":1}]}','gemini-3.5-flash-lite',NULL,NULL,3650,'success',NULL,'question_bank',5,'2026-11-12 03:40:23');
