INSERT INTO `questions` VALUES (1,1,1,'short','K2','CLO1','medium',5.0,'Explain the concept of physical data independence in a database management system, and provide a brief illustrative example of how it protects application programs.',NULL,'Physical data independence is the capacity to modify the internal schema (e.g., changing file organizations, indexing strategies, or storage structures) without having to alter the conceptual schema or existing application programs. For example, if a DBA decides to switch the underlying storage of a large employee table from an unindexed sequential file to a B+-tree indexed file, the application queries that retrieve employee records remain completely unaffected because the logical view provided by the conceptual schema does not change.','K2 level requires explaining a concept in one\'s own words and providing an example. This question targets physical data independence, explaining both the mechanism and its practical protective effect on applications.','Question: Explain the concept of physical data independence in a database management system, and provide a brief illustrative exam (5 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 1 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 1 mark(s)\nExample or application → 1 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 1 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 1 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-11 12:03:12'),(2,1,1,'short','K2','CLO1','medium',5.0,'Contrast the roles of the conceptual schema and the physical schema in the three-schema database architecture, using a practical database scenario to clarify their differences.',NULL,'The conceptual schema describes the overall database structure for all users, hiding the details of physical storage structures and focusing on entities, data types, relationships, and constraints. In contrast, the physical schema describes how the data is actually stored on secondary storage devices, including details regarding file structures, record placements, and compression or indexing techniques. For example, in a university database, the conceptual schema defines the \'Student\' entity with attributes like ID, Name, and GPA, whereas the physical schema specifies that the Student relation is stored as a clustered index on the student_id field within a specific disk block allocation.','Tests the understanding of schema layers in database architecture by asking for a comparison and a concrete scenario.','Question: Contrast the roles of the conceptual schema and the physical schema in the three-schema database architecture, using a p (5 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 1 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 1 mark(s)\nExample or application → 1 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 1 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 1 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-11 12:03:12'),(3,1,1,'short','K2','CLO1','medium',5.0,'Explain the significance of mapping cardinalities in an Entity-Relationship (ER) model, and describe how a one-to-many relationship is typically translated during the reduction to a relational schema.',NULL,'Mapping cardinalities express the maximum number of relationship instances that an entity can participate in, categorizing relationships as one-to-one, one-to-many, or many-to-many. During the reduction of a binary one-to-many relationship set from an ER diagram to a relational schema, the entity on the \'many\' side of the relationship is augmented with a foreign key that references the primary key of the entity on the \'one\' side. For example, in a Department-to-Employee one-to-many relationship, the Employee relation receives \'dept_id\' as a foreign key pointing to the Department relation.','Evaluates comprehension of ER modeling constraints and the algorithmic translation (reduction) process into relational schemas.','Question: Explain the significance of mapping cardinalities in an Entity-Relationship (ER) model, and describe how a one-to-many r (5 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 1 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 1 mark(s)\nExample or application → 1 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 1 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 1 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-11 12:03:12'),(4,1,1,'short','K2','CLO1','medium',5.0,'Discuss the primary purpose of database languages, specifically differentiating between Data Definition Language (DDL) and Data Manipulation Language (DML) with functional examples.',NULL,'Database languages are specialized languages used by database administrators and users to specify database schemas, express queries, and update data. DDL is used to define and modify the database structure and constraints, such as creating tables or altering columns (e.g., \'CREATE TABLE Instructor (ID INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(50));\'). DML, on the other hand, enables users to access or manipulate data organized by the appropriate data model, handling operations such as retrieval, insertion, deletion, and modification of actual records (e.g., \'INSERT INTO Instructor VALUES (101, \\\'Alice\\\');\').','Assesses the understanding of database system languages and the distinct functions of DDL versus DML.','Question: Discuss the primary purpose of database languages, specifically differentiating between Data Definition Language (DDL) a (5 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 1 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 1 mark(s)\nExample or application → 1 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 1 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 1 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-11 12:03:12'),(5,1,1,'short','K2','CLO1','hard',5.0,'Explain why weak entity sets require a supervising strong entity set, and describe how this dependency is represented in an ER diagram and resolved in a relational schema.',NULL,'A weak entity set is one that does not possess sufficient attributes to form a primary key of its own; thus, its existence depends on a strong entity set (referred to as the identifying owner). In an ER diagram, a weak entity is depicted with a double-lined rectangle, and its identifying relationship is shown with a double-lined diamond. When reducing this to a relational schema, the weak entity\'s relation includes its own partial discriminator attributes along with the primary key attributes of the strong entity set as a foreign key, with the combined set forming the primary key of the weak entity\'s relation.','Checks comprehension of weak entities, their notation in ER diagrams, and their structural conversion into relational tables.','Question: Explain why weak entity sets require a supervising strong entity set, and describe how this dependency is represented in (5 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 1 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 1 mark(s)\nExample or application → 1 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 1 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 1 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Database Management Systems\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-11 12:03:12'),(6,2,1,'short','K2','CLO1','medium',5.0,'Explain the primary difference between the OSI reference model and the TCP/IP reference model regarding their layer structures, providing an example of how a function in the transport layer is handled differently or similarly in both.',NULL,'The OSI model has 7 layers, separating the session and presentation layers distinctly from the application layer, whereas TCP/IP collapses these into a single application layer (4 layers total). Despite this, both models implement reliable end-to-end data transport (such as TCP) at their respective transport layers.','K2 requires explaining conceptual differences with examples. The student must demonstrate understanding of the structural divergence (7 vs 4 layers) and how protocol functions like reliable transport map across both architectures.','Question: Explain the primary difference between the OSI reference model and the TCP/IP reference model regarding their layer stru (5 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 1 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 1 mark(s)\nExample or application → 1 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 1 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 1 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:37:49'),(7,2,1,'short','K2','CLO1','medium',5.0,'Contrast guided transmission media with unguided transmission media by explaining how each guides signal propagation, and provide a real-world enterprise network scenario where combining both media types is necessary.',NULL,'Guided media (like fiber optics or twisted-pair copper) physically constrain electromagnetic signals along a solid path, offering high bandwidth and security. Unguided media (like wireless radio waves or microwaves) propagate signals through free space without a physical conductor, allowing mobility. An example scenario is a corporate office using fiber optics for high-speed backbone connections between wired desktop workstations and Wi-Fi access points for mobile laptop users.','This tests the student\'s ability to contrast physical propagation mechanisms and apply them to a practical network topology example.','Question: Contrast guided transmission media with unguided transmission media by explaining how each guides signal propagation, an (5 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 1 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 1 mark(s)\nExample or application → 1 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 1 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 1 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:37:49'),(8,2,1,'short','K2','CLO1','medium',5.0,'Describe the functional relationship between network software (specifically protocol stacks and operating systems) and network hardware (such as Network Interface Cards and routers). Give an example of how they interact when a packet is transmitted.',NULL,'Network hardware provides the physical components for signal transmission and reception, while network software (protocols and drivers) dictates how data is formatted, addressed, and controlled. For example, when an application sends data, the operating system\'s protocol stack encapsulates the data into packets and passes it to the NIC hardware, which translates those packets into electrical or optical signals for transmission over the physical medium.','The question targets the hardware-software interaction in Unit 1, requiring an explanation of encapsulation and hardware translation with an example.','Question: Describe the functional relationship between network software (specifically protocol stacks and operating systems) and n (5 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 1 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 1 mark(s)\nExample or application → 1 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 1 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 1 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:37:49'),(9,2,1,'short','K2','CLO1','medium',5.0,'Explain the concept of resource sharing as a primary use of computer networks, and distinguish between hardware resource sharing and software resource sharing using a campus network example.',NULL,'Resource sharing allows geographically distributed users to access common devices and programs efficiently. Hardware sharing involves multiple users utilizing a single physical device, such as a university department sharing a centralized high-end network printer. Software sharing involves accessing programs or data remotely, such as students accessing a centralized database license server or cloud-hosted integrated development environment over the campus network.','Evaluates the conceptual understanding of network uses (resource sharing) and requires clear examples differentiating hardware vs software domains.','Question: Explain the concept of resource sharing as a primary use of computer networks, and distinguish between hardware resource (5 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 1 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 1 mark(s)\nExample or application → 1 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 1 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 1 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:37:49'),(10,2,1,'short','K2','CLO1','medium',5.0,'Explain the role of the Physical Layer in the OSI model, focusing on how it handles bit representation, and give an example of how a digital bitstream is transformed for transmission over a copper cable.',NULL,'The Physical Layer is responsible for transmitting raw unstructured bits over a physical communication medium, managing voltage levels, data rates, and physical connectors. For example, to transmit a digital bitstream over a twisted-pair copper cable, the physical layer translates binary 1s and 0s into specific voltage fluctuations or electrical pulses (such as using Manchester encoding) so the receiving hardware can reliably interpret the signals.','Tests K2 understanding of the physical layer\'s core responsibility—bit transmission—and requires explaining the translation mechanism with an example.','Question: Explain the role of the Physical Layer in the OSI model, focusing on how it handles bit representation, and give an exam (5 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 1 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 1 mark(s)\nExample or application → 1 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 1 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 1 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:37:49'),(11,3,1,'mcq','K2','CLO1','medium',1.0,'How does the OSI reference model\'s Data Link layer handle error control differently than the Transport layer?','{\"A\":\"The Data Link layer provides node-to-node error detection and correction over a single physical link, whereas the Transport layer ensures end-to-end data integrity and reliability across the entire network path.\",\"B\":\"The Data Link layer retransmits lost packets across the entire internetwork, while the Transport layer only checks bit errors on the immediate physical wire.\",\"C\":\"The Data Link layer relies entirely on upper layers for framing and error detection, leaving the Transport layer responsible for MAC-address-based delivery.\",\"D\":\"The Data Link layer operates strictly in stateless mode without acknowledgments, while the Transport layer performs physical-medium modulation checks.\"}','A','At K2 level, understanding reference models requires distinguishing the scope of responsibilities between layers. The Data Link layer manages hop-by-hop framing and error control, while the Transport layer manages end-to-end reliability between source and destination hosts.','Correct option selected → 1 mark(s)\nIncorrect / blank → 0','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:39:07'),(12,3,1,'mcq','K2','CLO1','medium',1.0,'Why are multi-mode optical fibers typically used for short-range campus networks while single-mode fibers are preferred for long-distance telecommunications?','{\"A\":\"Multi-mode fiber has a larger core that allows multiple light modes to propagate, leading to modal dispersion over long distances, whereas single-mode fiber has a narrower core that supports a single light path with minimal dispersion.\",\"B\":\"Multi-mode fiber uses expensive laser diodes that can only transmit over short distances, whereas single-mode fiber uses cheap LEDs suitable for trans-oceanic cables.\",\"C\":\"Multi-mode fiber completely eliminates signal attenuation through amplification, while single-mode fiber suffers from high electromagnetic interference from power lines.\",\"D\":\"Multi-mode fiber operates exclusively on the principle of electrical conductivity, whereas single-mode fiber relies on wireless radio frequency coupling.\"}','A','This tests the understanding of guided transmission media characteristics. Core size and light propagation modes dictate that multi-mode suffers from modal dispersion over long runs, making single-mode necessary for high-bandwidth, long-distance links.','Correct option selected → 1 mark(s)\nIncorrect / blank → 0','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:39:07'),(13,3,1,'mcq','K2','CLO1','medium',1.0,'In the context of network software architecture, what is the primary structural difference between protocol stacks and layered operating systems?','{\"A\":\"Protocol stacks organize network functions into a vertical sequence of layers where each layer adds its own header to provide specific communication services, whereas traditional OS layering structures internal hardware management.\",\"B\":\"Protocol stacks combine all seven OSI layers into a single monolithic module for faster CPU execution, while layered operating systems separate network tasks into user-space applications.\",\"C\":\"Protocol stacks are hardware circuits embedded permanently in the NIC, whereas network software architecture refers exclusively to cloud storage protocols.\",\"D\":\"Protocol stacks eliminate the need for addressing by broadcasting all packets, whereas layered operating systems use centralized routing tables.\"}','A','K2 assessment of network software requires explaining how layering structures communication protocols through encapsulation and modular service provision, distinguishing it from general software constructs.','Correct option selected → 1 mark(s)\nIncorrect / blank → 0','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:39:07'),(14,3,1,'mcq','K2','CLO1','medium',1.0,'How do connection-oriented services in the TCP/IP suite differ conceptually from connectionless services?','{\"A\":\"Connection-oriented services establish a formal virtual circuit or handshake before data transfer to guarantee ordered delivery, whereas connectionless services treat each packet independently without prior setup.\",\"B\":\"Connection-oriented services route packets through dedicated physical copper wires for the duration of the session, whereas connectionless services use optical fiber exclusively.\",\"C\":\"Connection-oriented services do not use sequence numbers because reliability is handled entirely by the physical layer, whereas connectionless services require complex handshakes.\",\"D\":\"Connection-oriented services are restricted to local area networks, whereas connectionless services are only applicable to peer-to-peer file sharing.\"}','A','This evaluates the conceptual understanding of network services. Connection-oriented protocols (like TCP) maintain state and sequencing, whereas connectionless protocols (like UDP/IP) route datagrams independently.','Correct option selected → 1 mark(s)\nIncorrect / blank → 0','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:39:07'),(15,3,1,'mcq','K2','CLO1','medium',1.0,'When comparing unguided transmission media, why is line-of-sight propagation critical for microwave communication frequencies above 10 GHz?','{\"A\":\"Higher frequency waves are easily blocked by physical obstacles and experience severe atmospheric absorption, preventing reliable diffraction around the curvature of the Earth.\",\"B\":\"Higher frequency signals require physical metallic wave-guides laid out along the ground to prevent them from escaping into the upper atmosphere.\",\"C\":\"Higher frequency radio waves travel faster than the speed of light in a vacuum, requiring direct alignment to prevent temporal distortion.\",\"D\":\"Higher frequency transmissions automatically convert into guided infrared beams once they travel more than ten meters from the transmitter.\"}','A','Testing unguided media properties at K2 level involves explaining how frequency bands dictate propagation methods, specifically why high-frequency microwaves demand unobstructed line-of-sight paths.','Correct option selected → 1 mark(s)\nIncorrect / blank → 0','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:39:07'),(16,4,1,'long','K2','CLO1','hard',10.0,'Compare and contrast the OSI reference model and the TCP/IP reference model in terms of their layer structures, design philosophies, and how they handle the transport and network layers. Provide specific architectural examples for each model to illustrate your explanation.',NULL,'The OSI model is a 7-layer conceptual framework developed by ISO, strictly separating concerns with distinct presentation and session layers, but can be rigid. The TCP/IP model is a 4-layer pragmatic suite developed by DoD, combining presentation and session into the application layer and keeping link-layer details separate from internetworking. For example, TCP/IP successfully integrated IP as a universal network-layer protocol across diverse hardware, whereas OSI\'s rigorous theoretical approach often led to over-engineered specifications.','This question requires a comprehensive K2-level explanation mapping structural differences and practical examples between OSI and TCP/IP without circular definitions.','Question: Compare and contrast the OSI reference model and the TCP/IP reference model in terms of their layer structures, design p (10 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 2 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 2 mark(s)\nExample or application → 2 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 2 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 2 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:39:44'),(17,4,1,'long','K2','CLO1','hard',10.0,'Explain the fundamental differences between guided (wired) and unguided (wireless) transmission media, focusing on twisted-pair cables, coaxial cables, fiber optics, and radio/microwave propagation. Include real-world operational examples and limitations for each medium.',NULL,'Guided media use physical conductors to confine signals, such as copper strands in twisted-pair and coaxial cables for local area networks and cable television, or glass cores in fiber optics for high-speed, long-distance backbone connections utilizing total internal reflection. Unguided media transmit electromagnetic waves through free space via antennas, such as terrestrial microwave and radio frequencies used in Wi-Fi and cellular networks, which are subject to environmental interference, attenuation, and security vulnerabilities compared to physical cables.','Tests understanding of physical layer transmission characteristics, operational principles, and practical trade-offs.','Question: Explain the fundamental differences between guided (wired) and unguided (wireless) transmission media, focusing on twist (10 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 2 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 2 mark(s)\nExample or application → 2 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 2 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 2 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:39:44'),(18,4,1,'long','K2','CLO1','hard',10.0,'Discuss the architectural concept of protocol layering and service interfaces in computer networks. Explain how peer-to-peer communication occurs across different layers using headers, encapsulation, and decapsulation, supported by a concrete end-to-end data transfer example.',NULL,'Protocol layering breaks complex network communication into manageable, modular vertical tiers where each layer provides services to the layer above it while hiding implementation details. As data moves down the sender\'s stack, each layer prepends its own control header via encapsulation. Upon reaching the receiver, the peer layer strips its respective header via decapsulation. For instance, an HTTP request gets a TCP segment header, then an IP packet header, and finally a data-link frame header before physical transmission, reversing the process at the destination.','Evaluates the candidate\'s conceptual grasp of modular network software design and data encapsulation mechanics.','Question: Discuss the architectural concept of protocol layering and service interfaces in computer networks. Explain how peer-to- (10 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 2 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 2 mark(s)\nExample or application → 2 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 2 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 2 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:39:44'),(19,4,1,'long','K2','CLO1','hard',10.0,'Examine the classification of computer networks based on their geographical scale, specifically contrasting Local Area Networks (LANs), Metropolitan Area Networks (MANs), and Wide Area Networks (WANs). Discuss the typical hardware components, transmission technologies, and ownership models associated with each category.',NULL,'LANs cover a single room, building, or campus, utilizing high-speed Ethernet switches and private coaxial or twisted-pair media with high data rates and low delay, typically owned by a single organization. MANs span an entire city or town, often bridging multiple LANs using technologies like fiber-optic rings or carrier Ethernet. WANs span countries or continents, interconnecting disparate networks via leased telecommunication lines, routers, satellite links, and optical transport networks, often managed by multiple telecom service providers.','Tests network topologies and geographical categorizations alongside underlying hardware architectures.','Question: Examine the classification of computer networks based on their geographical scale, specifically contrasting Local Area N (10 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 2 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 2 mark(s)\nExample or application → 2 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 2 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 2 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:39:44'),(20,4,1,'long','K2','CLO1','hard',10.0,'Explain the role and functionality of core network hardware devices—including repeaters, hubs, bridges, switches, routers, and gateways—within the context of the physical and data link layers of the OSI reference model. Provide examples of how each device manages collision domains and broadcast domains.',NULL,'Repeaters and hubs operate at the physical layer, blindly amplifying or repeating electrical signals across all ports, thus sharing a single collision and broadcast domain. Bridges and layer-2 switches operate at the data link layer, inspecting MAC addresses to forward frames selectively, thereby segmenting collision domains while typically maintaining a single broadcast domain. Routers operate at the network layer using IP addresses to route packets between different networks, breaking up both collision and broadcast domains. Gateways operate across multiple layers to perform protocol translation between incompatible architectures.','Requires clear articulation of network device functionalities, OSI layer placement, and domain segmentation concepts.','Question: Explain the role and functionality of core network hardware devices—including repeaters, hubs, bridges, switches, router (10 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 2 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 2 mark(s)\nExample or application → 2 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 2 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 2 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:39:44'),(21,5,1,'essay','K2','CLO1','hard',10.0,'Compare and contrast the OSI reference model and the TCP/IP reference model. Explain two structural similarities and two key architectural differences between them, providing a practical networking example for each difference.',NULL,'Model outline: Similarities include layered architecture promoting modularity and having distinct transport and network layers. Differences include OSI having 7 layers with strict service/protocol separation while TCP/IP has 4 layers blending presentation/session into application and data link/physical into link layer. Examples should illustrate how TCP/IP bundles protocols like HTTP/TLS/DNS into the application layer compared to OSI\'s distinct layers.',NULL,'Question: Compare and contrast the OSI reference model and the TCP/IP reference model. Explain two structural similarities and two (10 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 2 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 2 mark(s)\nExample or application → 2 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 2 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 2 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:40:23'),(22,5,1,'essay','K2','CLO1','medium',10.0,'Explain the fundamental differences between guided (wired) and unguided (wireless) transmission media in terms of signal propagation, attenuation, and susceptibility to environmental interference. Provide real-world networking scenarios where each medium is uniquely advantageous.',NULL,'Model outline: Guided media (e.g., twisted pair, fiber optics) constrain signals within a physical path, typically offering higher bandwidth and lower interference, ideal for high-speed local data centers. Unguided media (e.g., radio waves, microwaves) propagate signals through free space, susceptible to atmospheric attenuation, multipath fading, and interference, but essential for mobile and remote connectivity.',NULL,'Question: Explain the fundamental differences between guided (wired) and unguided (wireless) transmission media in terms of signal (10 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 2 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 2 mark(s)\nExample or application → 2 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 2 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 2 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:40:23'),(23,5,1,'essay','K2','CLO1','medium',10.0,'Discuss the hierarchical organization of network hardware and software. Explain the roles of edge devices (hosts), intermediate devices (routers and switches), and how the operating system\'s network stack interacts with protocol software to move data across a network.',NULL,'Model outline: Edge devices generate and consume user data. Intermediate devices forward packets across network boundaries using switching and routing functions. Software architecture separates user applications from kernel-level protocol stacks, where data is encapsulated as it descends through layers and decapsulated upon receipt, demonstrating clean separation of concerns.',NULL,'Question: Discuss the hierarchical organization of network hardware and software. Explain the roles of edge devices (hosts), inter (10 marks)\n\nMarking Scheme:\nKey concept / definition → 2 mark(s)\nCorrect explanation / reasoning → 2 mark(s)\nExample or application → 2 mark(s)\nStructure / completeness → 2 mark(s)\nConclusion / accuracy → 2 mark(s)','{\"subject\":\"Computer Networks\",\"clo_code\":\"CLO1\"}','2026-11-12 03:40:23');
